export default "This guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## The Retrospective Nature of Menopause\n\nThere is a particular challenge in identifying the last period: you cannot recognize it while it is happening. Unlike other health milestones, menopause has no real-time signal. The last period looks like any other late-perimenopause bleed. It may be irregular, perhaps lighter than before, perhaps longer delayed than the previous one. Only 12 months of silence confirms it was the last.\n\nThis is not a limitation of current medicine. It is the nature of the hormonal process itself. Estrogen production does not switch off in a single event. It declines gradually, with fluctuations, until the ovaries are no longer producing enough to sustain the endometrial cycle.\n\n## What the Period Pattern Looks Like Before Menopause\n\n### Years before the final period\n\nThe menopausal transition, perimenopause, typically begins 4 to 8 years before the final period. The earliest sign for many people is increasing cycle variability. Cycles that were once reliably 26 to 30 days may begin running 20 days one month and 40 days the next.\n\nSome people first notice that premenstrual symptoms, particularly mood symptoms, are worsening. Others first notice hot flashes, which can begin years before significant cycle changes occur.\n\n### The approach to the final period\n\nAs perimenopause advances, gaps between periods lengthen. A person might go 45 days, then 60 days, then 3 months between bleeds. Then have a few regular cycles. Then skip another 4 months. The pattern is not a linear wind-down; it is irregular until it stops.\n\nFlow often changes during this phase. Some periods are heavier than before (related to anovulatory cycles and unopposed estrogen building up the uterine lining). Others are lighter. Some are just spotting.\n\n### After the last period\n\nOnce 12 consecutive months without any vaginal bleeding have passed, menopause is confirmed. Any bleeding after that point, even light spotting, is postmenopausal bleeding and should be evaluated promptly by a healthcare provider. Postmenopausal bleeding is not a period returning. It requires investigation to rule out endometrial pathology.\n\n## Why Tracking the Transition Matters\n\nWithout a record, the perimenopausal pattern is very difficult to interpret. Periods that come every 6 to 8 weeks feel vaguely irregular but not dramatically so. After a year, without a log, you may not remember whether that last bleed was 10 months ago or 14 months ago.\n\nWith a record, you can:\n- See the progression of gap lengthening over time\n- Identify the date of any bleed precisely, which is necessary for the 12-month calculation\n- Document symptom changes (hot flash onset, sleep changes, mood, vaginal dryness) relative to cycle changes\n- Provide specific information to a provider rather than general impressions\n\n### What to track during perimenopause and through menopause\n\n- Period start and end dates, even for very light or short bleeds\n- Flow intensity each day\n- Any spotting (separate from a period)\n- Hot flash frequency and intensity\n- Night sweats and their effect on sleep\n- Mood, anxiety, or depression\n- Vaginal dryness or discomfort\n- Any new physical symptoms\n\nThis is particularly important in the years after the last period, when the 12-month clock is running. You need to know whether any bleeding has occurred during that period. A spotting event you do not log may not be remembered clearly when you are trying to confirm menopause status.\n\n## Health Changes After Menopause\n\nLower estrogen levels after menopause affect more than the menstrual cycle. Areas that become more important to monitor and discuss with a provider include:\n\n- **Bone density:** Estrogen has a protective effect on bone. Postmenopausal decline in estrogen is associated with increased bone loss. Bone density assessment (DEXA scan) is commonly recommended at or after menopause.\n- **Cardiovascular health:** The protective cardiovascular effects of estrogen diminish after menopause. Lipid profiles and blood pressure monitoring become more important.\n- **Urogenital health:** Vaginal atrophy and urinary symptoms (including increased urgency and recurrent UTIs) are common postmenopause and respond well to treatment, including local estrogen therapy.\n\nThese changes are part of what makes the menopausal transition worth discussing with a healthcare provider rather than simply waiting through.\n\n## Menopausal Transition Data and Privacy\n\nHealth data generated during the menopausal transition is sensitive. It reveals age-related health information, detailed symptom profiles, and reproductive history spanning years. For people in workplaces where health status could be perceived as relevant, or for anyone who values controlling their own medical records, how tracking data is stored is not a trivial question.\n\nPeriod tracker apps that store data on company servers create long-term records: years of cycle data, symptom entries, gap durations, and the precise date of the final period. That data is not covered by HIPAA. It can be accessed by the company, shared with third parties, or exposed in a breach.\n\nAn app that stores data only on your device has no record to share and no server to breach. For a multi-year health transition, that architectural choice determines whether your health history stays with you or becomes part of a company's data asset.";
